WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.480
Analysts turned to
historical aircraft

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:05.870
designs for clues to the
monster plane's purpose.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.380
There's a really deep
history in aviation of

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:12.350
building gigantic
aircraft that pushed the

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.210
limits of what we think
is possible.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.820
And the most iconic of
these is the Spruce

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:17.600
Goose.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.030
The colossal 320 foot
wingspan.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:23.990
Spruce Goose is followed
three decades later by

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.440
the Antonov 225, which
holds the world record

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.070
for the biggest ever
payload equivalent to 45

00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:36.230
African elephants. But
the aircraft in the image

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:38.960
has a very different
design from these monster

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:39.770
planes.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.990
The Antonov has a very
large fuselage to carry

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.820
large amounts of stuff.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.760
When we look at our
mystery aircraft, it has

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:53.060
two fuselages, both of
which are very skinny.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.730
It looks more like it's
built to carry something

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.260
underneath the aircraft.

